#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0a725b9-f580-5784-b0b9-e149e4e72fb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370509} {:answer/id #uuid "4982bee4-e258-5bee-ac44-60ecfa556299" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370510} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ee4366-02d0-5ffc-ad7e-97da5bd8acbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370511} {:answer/id #uuid "37b67e26-88ae-54bc-ab2e-d4e87f9c340b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370512} {:answer/id #uuid "90651eaf-c146-532f-9cb5-80f591989c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90651eaf-c146-532f-9cb5-80f591989c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370513} :answer-field/id #uuid "a589bdb3-81ae-5bb4-9120-de1332971711" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6920e043-98c8-591f-ba7e-c0a9f1bbfc02" :question/math-academy-id "q-141159" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=⟨b,a⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in R$\n- $⟨a,a⟩>0$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is true. For any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\overset{―}{\\alpha }=\\alpha .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $a=0,$ then $⟨a,a⟩=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ffdcda1-c24e-51e4-943d-d5f7ff7f37a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8+7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334268} {:answer/id #uuid "21a46b06-586d-5437-aaf9-1bb3455accfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334269} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb4f2a1-62fb-51f0-a87d-b802ece00745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7-4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334270} {:answer/id #uuid "ac56175f-02af-5936-8661-96f49b5d8a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7+8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334271} {:answer/id #uuid "d5db2c9e-ec3c-5cd5-95a7-d523056e0a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7+4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac56175f-02af-5936-8661-96f49b5d8a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7+8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334271} :answer-field/id #uuid "50a2b0a5-dc01-5c4b-be7f-8e93bb04a234" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0f3a852-f21d-5ba1-9973-db675afd165e" :question/math-academy-id "q-141161" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨2a+3b,\\,c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨c,a⟩=2-\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨b,c⟩=1+2\\text{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨2a+3b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨2a,c⟩+⟨3b,c⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨a,c⟩+3⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2⟨a,c⟩+3⟨b,c⟩ & =2\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}+3⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨c,a⟩$ and $⟨b,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}+3⟨b,c⟩ & =2(\\overset{―}{2-\\text{i}})+3(1+2\\text{i}) \\\\  & =2(2+\\text{i})+3(1+2\\text{i}) \\\\  & =4+2\\text{i}+3+6\\text{i} \\\\  & =7+8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4509992-50c1-5828-ab5b-07250578b3a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10+8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334310} {:answer/id #uuid "31b5d283-2a55-541e-ae49-bd7e65c9d3e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334311} {:answer/id #uuid "75364442-42c3-561f-b9ac-c519065bafd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6+8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334312} {:answer/id #uuid "3e95a633-20e9-5d84-8b07-c79f45acfa9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5+6\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334313} {:answer/id #uuid "76940e5b-58bc-5dbd-8b37-71eeea87b239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31b5d283-2a55-541e-ae49-bd7e65c9d3e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334311} :answer-field/id #uuid "952730dc-add8-574d-835c-407db745d23a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6140addf-ac61-581c-af38-58b12a2e258d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97167" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨2a-b,\\,c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,c⟩=8+\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨c,b⟩=4-5\\text{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨2a-b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨2a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2⟨a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩ & =2⟨a,c⟩-\\overset{―}{⟨c,b}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨a,c⟩$ and $⟨c,b⟩:$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2⟨a,c⟩-\\overset{―}{⟨c,b}⟩ & =2(8+\\text{i})-(\\overset{―}{4-5\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =2(8+\\text{i})-(4+5\\text{i}) \\\\  & =16+2\\text{i}-4-5\\text{i} \\\\  & =12-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4aa4667-1865-5528-933f-5bc88576414f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334317} {:answer/id #uuid "16ce4e59-5862-5e14-836a-f4d84baebc40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334318} {:answer/id #uuid "e4622167-cb75-5159-a99f-28399d0d21cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334319} {:answer/id #uuid "ec818b58-543a-5d33-8c6e-65bfcc04a757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334320} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1a0728-b6ce-5cc5-8627-6825e57c538a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16ce4e59-5862-5e14-836a-f4d84baebc40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334318} :answer-field/id #uuid "16991709-53aa-52e3-b461-bee5e6b8d6bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ceaeef65-5cc5-5bd2-865b-0a244110fe3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-97303" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨b,a⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨a,b⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $i⟨a,b⟩=⟨-ia,b⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is false. A complex  inner product is linear  in the first argument,  so we have \n\n$$\n⟨-ia,b⟩=-i⟨a,b⟩\\ne i⟨a,b⟩,\n$$\n\nin general.\n\n- Statement III is true. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$  $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5eb8ef4-3721-5595-b3d0-70c0ac20bed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133275} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9d0144-2372-5db3-a5e8-9c3f3ff38083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133276} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc5f085-4be9-516e-9989-ec325a733b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133277} {:answer/id #uuid "5abcb316-87c8-59a5-90c3-48e886fd1389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133278} {:answer/id #uuid "73513a08-ff04-5422-b16f-4936b5fe18b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73513a08-ff04-5422-b16f-4936b5fe18b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133279} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fdeb67d-1107-512b-8388-e230a103fbac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f527ee5b-22e1-5696-9bd4-0e444190bb44" :question/math-academy-id "q-97305" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,0⟩=0$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $2⟨a,b⟩=⟨a,-2b⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. By the antilinearity in the second argument we have that  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,0⟩ & =⟨a,0\\cdot 0⟩=\\overset{―}{0}⟨a,0⟩=0⟨a,0⟩=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. By the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,-2b⟩=\\overset{―}{(-2)}⟨a,b⟩=-2⟨a,b⟩\\ne 2⟨a,b⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number, in general.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
